{"name":"Blue Ribbon","hex":"#0066ff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue ribbon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The highest honor or prize awarded in some competitions and contests.","The emblem of the Order of the Garter."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Highly superior in quality, style, substance, prestige, etc.","Pertaining to the Liberal Party, its supporters and regions."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_ribbon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue ribbon","blocks":[{"text":"Blue ribbons are typically a symbol of high quality. The association comes from The Blue Riband, a prize awarded for the fastest crossing of the Atlantic Ocean by passenger liners and, prior to that from Cordon Bleu, which referred to the blue ribbon worn by the French knightly Order of the Holy Spirit.\nThe spelling \"blue riband\" is still encountered in most English-speaking countries, but in the United States, the term was altered to blue ribbon, and ribbons of this color came to be awarded for first place in certain athletic or other competitive endeavors."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_ribbon","wikidata":"Q3315947"},{"title":"Blue Ribbon (software house)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Ribbon was the budget computer software publishing label of CDS Micro Systems."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Ribbon_(software_house)","wikidata":"Q4929669"},{"title":"Ribbon","blocks":[{"text":"A ribbon is a thin band of material, typically cloth but also plastic or sometimes metal, used primarily as decorative binding and tying. Cloth ribbons are made of natural materials such as silk, cotton, and jute and of synthetic materials, such as polyester, nylon, and polypropylene. Ribbon is used for useful, ornamental, and symbolic purposes. Cultures around the world use ribbon in their hair, around the body, and as ornament on non-human animals, buildings, and packaging. Some popular fabrics used to make ribbons are satin, organza, sheer, silk, velvet, and grosgrain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg/330px-Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbon","wikidata":"Q6918773"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Moe_Epsilon%27s_blue_ribbon.jpg/330px-Moe_Epsilon%27s_blue_ribbon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Moe_Epsilon%27s_blue_ribbon.jpg/330px-Moe_Epsilon%27s_blue_ribbon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A blue ribbon won at a convention","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoe_Epsilon's_blue_ribbon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Glaube.jpg/500px-Glaube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com